# Description

Improves module to allow Rust to access platform-provided data paths.

**iOS**

<img width="1832" height="306" alt="ios-data-path" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a82bced3-27ae-40fa-98d4-3ae39a9d62e9" />

**Android**

<img width="2643" height="1076" alt="android-data-path" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/65d3227a-1757-43c5-b14d-7825402472bf" />

On iOS, platform APIs can be accessed immediately from the Objective-C environment. However, on Android, due to AutoLinking implementation constraints, `Context` is unavailable during package loading.

To work around this (see the 'No. 2' badge), we use a tricky approach to access `Context` by exploiting the TurboModule Provider implementation. (This triggers an error log in Logcat: `getOrCreateModule(): Unable to create module "__crabyXXX_JNI_prepare__" (legacy: false, turbo: true)`, but it doesn't affect functionality.)

**Rust implemantation**

```rust
impl CrabyTest {
    fn get_file_path(&self) -> PathBuf {
        // `self.ctx.data_path` is application's data path
        PathBuf::from(self.ctx.data_path.clone()).join("data.txt")
    }
}

impl CrabyTestSpec for CrabyTest {
    fn get_data_path(&mut self) -> String {
        self.ctx.data_path.clone()
    }

    fn write_data(&mut self, value: &str) -> Boolean {
        std::fs::write(self.get_file_path(), value).is_ok()
    }

    fn read_data(&mut self) -> Nullable<String> {
        match std::fs::read_to_string(self.get_file_path()) {
            Ok(data) => Nullable::<String>::some(data),
            Err(_) => Nullable::<String>::none(),
        }
    }
}
```


